  ],
  "text": "Rivers carry salt to the sea, and never carry a single grain back. River water tastes like nothing. So where is the sea getting it from? Rain is very slightly acidic, and it slowly eats rock. So every river is quietly carrying dissolved rock downhill, all day, forever. It all arrives in the sea. And then the water leaves without it. Evaporation only ever takes the water. Salt is not light enough to follow. And volcanic vents on the seafloor keep quietly adding more from below. So the sea is not salty. It is an accumulator nobody has ever emptied. Rivers are salty too. Just diluted enough that you have never once noticed. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
